---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import ThinnerContent from "../../components/ThinnerContent.astro";
import Back from "../../components/Back.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import heroImage from "../../assets/WestMarine.jpg";

const title = "West Marine";
---

<Base title={title}>
  <ThinnerContent>
    <section class="mb-16">

      <!-- Header -->
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase text-violet-800 mt-8 mb-2">
        Case Study
      </p>
      <h1 class="font-heading font-bold text-4xl sm:text-5xl mb-10">{title}</h1>

      <!-- Hero -->
      <Image
        src={heroImage}
        alt="West Marine catalog prepress"
        class="w-full rounded-lg mb-10"
      />

      <!-- Overview -->
      <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-heading font-bold text-2xl mb-4">Overview</h2>
        <p class="mb-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
          Before I arrived, West Marine&rsquo;s 25-person creative team was sending every page of their
          monthly mailers and 600+ page annual catalog to an outside agency, waiting days for printed
          proofs, marking them up, and FedExing them back. It was slow, expensive, and out of their
          hands. I was hired to bring prepress in-house and save the company millions in agency fees.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
          Every page started coming through me before it went to press. I&rsquo;d read it the way an
          editor reads a manuscript, except for the things that only show up in print: type consistency,
          kerning, widows and orphans, rich blacks, neutral grays, four-color builds on small type, image
          resolution and distortion, overset text. Then each page went out to PDF/X and through a custom
          Enfocus PitStop Pro preflight profile I built to catch whatever my eyes had missed. Stakeholder
          proofing rounds that used to take a week now took a few hours, and when everything was signed
          off I uploaded the final files through the printer&rsquo;s portal.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
          Three or four times a year I flew to the Midwest to press check the catalog, standing on the
          pressroom floor with the operators as the first sheets came off. Those trips built the kind of
          relationships with printers and paper and print brokers where a phone call solved a problem
          before it became one. Seven years in, the team had a proofing system they could trust, real
          control over how their work looked in print, and millions of dollars that stayed in the
          building.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- TODO: add catalog scan gallery -->

    </section>
    <Back next="/work/fastpencil" />
  </ThinnerContent>
</Base>
